**Option 1 — Soundness of resolution** ($F \vdash \varphi \Rightarrow F \models \varphi$). Prove each rule sound (if $a$ satisfies the premises it satisfies the conclusion) using the substitution lemma on generalized clauses:
- falsehood: $\varphi_i[\varnothing/\bot] \equiv \chi \equiv \bot \lor \chi$ (same for $\lnot\top$);
- double negation: $\varphi_i[\psi/\lnot\lnot\psi] \equiv \psi \lor \chi \equiv \varphi_i$;
- α: $\varphi_i[\alpha_1/\alpha] \land \varphi_i[\alpha_2/\alpha] \equiv (\alpha_1 \land \alpha_2) \lor \chi \equiv \varphi_i$;
- β: $\varphi_i[\beta_1,\beta_2/\beta] \equiv (\beta_1 \lor \beta_2) \lor \psi \equiv \varphi_i$;
- resolution: with $\varphi_i \equiv \psi \lor \psi_i$, $\varphi_j \equiv \lnot\psi \lor \psi_j$: if $a \models \psi$ then $a \models \psi_j$, else $a \models \psi_i$, so $a \models \psi_i \lor \psi_j$;
- cut: $a \models \psi \lor \lnot\psi$ always.

So a satisfying valuation satisfies every expansion; a closed expansion contains $\varnothing$, which is unsatisfiable; $F \vdash \varphi$ means $F \cup \{\lnot\varphi\}$ has a closed expansion, hence is unsatisfiable, i.e. $F \models \varphi$. $\square$

**Option 2 — Theorem (Compactness).** $F \subseteq L$ is satisfiable iff every finite subset of $F$ is.

*Proof.* Necessity: a valuation satisfying $F$ satisfies every subset. Sufficiency: let $C \subseteq \mathcal{P}L$ be the class of sets $S$ **all of whose finite subsets are satisfiable**; show $C$ is a consistency poset.
1. If $S \in C$ and $x \in S$: $\{x,\lnot x\}$ is finite and unsatisfiable, so it is not $\subseteq S$; hence $\lnot x \notin S$.
2. $\{\bot\}$ and $\{\lnot\top\}$ are finite unsatisfiable, so $\bot, \lnot\top \notin S$.
3. $\lnot\lnot\varphi \in S \in C$: a finite $T \subseteq S \cup \{\varphi\}$ lies in $U \cup \{\varphi\}$ for some finite $U \subseteq S$ containing $\lnot\lnot\varphi$; $U$ is satisfied by some $v$, and $v(\varphi) = v(\lnot\lnot\varphi) = 1$, so $v \models T$. Thus $S \cup \{\varphi\} \in C$.
4. $\alpha \in S \in C$: a finite $T \subseteq S \cup \{\alpha_1,\alpha_2\}$ lies in $U \cup \{\alpha_1,\alpha_2\}$, $U \ni \alpha$ finite; $v \models U$ gives $v \models \alpha$, hence $v \models \alpha_1,\alpha_2$, so $v \models T$.
5. $\beta \in S \in C$: if neither $S \cup \{\beta_1\}$ nor $S \cup \{\beta_2\}$ is in $C$, there are finite $T_i \subseteq S$ with $T_i \cup \{\beta_i\}$ unsatisfiable (WLOG $\beta \in T_i$). $T := T_1 \cup T_2$ is a finite subset of $S$, so some $v \models T$; then $v \models \beta$, so $v \models \beta_i$ for some $i$, and $v \models T_i \cup \{\beta_i\}$ — contradiction.

Every element of a consistency poset is satisfiable; since all finite subsets of $F$ are satisfiable, $F \in C$, so $F$ is satisfiable. $\square$
